{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>וישלחהו.</b> Every [word of the pattern] shaluach followed by a mem [suffix] is like גרוש, and it is pejorative, like שלח מעל פני (Jeremiah 15:1). And not as a great sage of Spain said, that it is an expression of honor, like עמם לשלחם (Genesis 18:16), and like וישלחהו וילך בשלום (II Samuel 3:23) — for if so, the honor of the ground would be greater than the honor of the Garden of Eden. And if you say: why was ויגרש את האדם written? [the answer is:] if the meaning of וישלחהו is ויגרשהו, such is its meaning: just as he drove him out, he stationed the cherubim." "And the word כרובים — some say [it means] like youths; the Aramaic translation is כרביא. In my opinion it is a word [of the root] לל [meaning] forms. And Ezekiel, who said at first ופני שור — no, the form had changed, because the calf was not made in his days; and afterward, when he says פני הכרוב (Ezekiel 10:14) with the definite heh, the form is the form of an ox, as though he said: the form that I mentioned at first — therefore every ox-form is a cherub, but not every cherub is an ox-form. And the complete proof is that he said at the end כי כרובים המה (Ezekiel 10:20). And here [scripture] says הכרובים, and they are the known angels, and in their hand is a sword; להט has two meanings, and this is the meaning of המתהפכת. And the commentators who say it refers to the sun — it is not true."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "Resolved 'a great sage of Spain' as referring to an unidentified Spanish exegete; no standard epithet to resolve further.", 1 "The phrase 'לל צורות כולם' is cryptic; translated hyper-literally. The argument appears to involve a root analysis of כרובים but the syntax is compressed and possibly textually difficult."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:12.826885405Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}